<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banda Aceh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kutaraja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> is a port city at the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, near the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. As the capital of the Aceh Sultanate from the sixteenth century it dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule after 1874 it was called Kutaraja. Nearby lay the earlier harbour kingdom of Lamuri. The city was devastated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> tsunami of 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banda-aceh.jpg" alt="Banda Aceh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Si Gam · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meuseujid_Raya_Baiturrahman,_Aceh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> lies on the northwestern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-river">Aceh River</a>, at an elevation of 35 metres. It is a semi-enclave within Aceh Besar Regency, bordering the Andaman Sea to the northwest. The first part of its name comes from the Persian bandar, meaning port or haven. It has been called the porch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, Serambi Mekkah, from the days when pilgrims travelling by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> stopped there before continuing to Mecca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Banda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> has long been a strategic transport and trading hub in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Marco Polo visited in 1292 and called it Lambri, from the Lamuri Kingdom that existed there, noting it as the logical first port of call for travellers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. By the mid-14th century it was under the trading kingdom of Samudera Pasai, which collapsed under declining economic conditions and pressure from the Portuguese, who occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in the early 16th century. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mughayat-syah">Ali Mughayat Syah</a> of the newly founded Sultanate of Aceh expanded aggressively in the 1520s, built the sultanate on the remains of Pasai and other extinct kingdoms, and made the city its capital.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> on 26 March 1873 and bombarded the capital. With British support the city had been modernised and fortified, and the Dutch underestimated its defences. Their commander, General Johan Köhler, was killed in a skirmish near the city and the first expedition failed. A second expedition within months overwhelmed it, and the Dutch entered the capital in January 1874 believing the Acehnese had surrendered, but the conflict moved into the countryside. The city's name was changed back to Banda Aceh on 9 May 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Banda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> is the capital and largest city of the Indonesian province of Aceh. On 26 December 2004 a tsunami caused by a 9.2-magnitude earthquake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> struck the city, which was the closest major city to the epicentre. It destroyed more than 60 per cent of the city's buildings, and figures for the dead run from 60,000 to 167,000 inhabitants. Memorials include mass graves at Ulee Lheue, the Tsunami Museum and PLTD Apung 1, a floating diesel power ship carried inland. The Baiturrahman Grand Mosque, built in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> (1607 to 1636), was rebuilt in 1875 after burning in the Aceh war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banda Aceh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His capital, <b>Bandar Aceh Darussalam</b> near the mouth of the Aceh River, later called Kutaraja by the Dutch and now Banda Aceh, became the seat of a dynasty that identified itself firmly with Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay near modern <b>Banda Aceh</b>, and the archaeological site of Lamreh on the coast has revealed a fortified settlement and early Muslim tombstones.</blockquote><a href="/state/lamuri">Lamuri</a></li></ul></section>
